window.EXERCISE_DB.exam.court.en.push({
  id: 30,
  title: "The Law of Injunctions and Equitable Discretion",
  text: "An injunction represents an extraordinary equitable remedy granted by civil courts to restrain the commission, repetition, or continuance of a wrongful act, or to compel the performance of a mandatory legal obligation. Because injunctive relief originates from chancery jurisprudence of conscience and equity rather than strict common law, the grant of an injunction remains entirely discretionary for the presiding judicial officer. A court will not grant injunctive relief where an adequate, efficacious, and complete remedy can be obtained through ordinary damages or statutory compensation. In applications for temporary injunction, the plaintiff must satisfy three interdependent legal tests: the demonstration of a strong prima facie case, proof that irreparable injury will occur if the injunction is refused, and showing that the balance of convenience tilts decisively in their favor. Irreparable injury does not mean physical injury that cannot be repaired, but rather material legal harm that cannot be adequately quantified or redressed through monetary damages. Furthermore, the party seeking equity must approach the court with clean hands, having disclosed all material facts candidly without suppression or bad faith. The judicious exercise of injunctive powers preserves property status quo, prevents irreversible structural waste, and maintains civil order during ongoing litigation."
});
